# cuems-common .link files — hardware-specific configuration

cuems-common ships `etc/systemd/network/10-*.link` files that rename
PCI ethernet/wireless NICs to stable names (`ethernet0`, `ethernet1`,
`wifi0`) so the rest of the package (bond config, dhcpd interface,
hostapd, etc) can reference them.

The default values in the shipped package match the **N97 cluster
node** hardware:

| Logical name | PCI path             | Notes                                    |
|--------------|----------------------|------------------------------------------|
| `ethernet0`  | `pci-0000:01:00.0`   | Primary NIC, bond0 primary on controllers |
| `ethernet1`  | `pci-0000:02:00.0`   | Cluster ipv4ll segment                    |
| `wifi0`      | `pci-0000:03:00.0`   | Controllers only; nodes have no wifi NIC |

## If your hardware has different PCI paths

**Check first** — `ls -l /sys/class/net/*/device | grep -v lo` will
show the actual PCI BDF for each NIC.

If the package defaults don't match your topology, you MUST edit
`/etc/systemd/network/10-*.link` to use your host's actual PCI paths
**before** rebooting. Otherwise udev's `net_setup_link` builtin will
fail to match, fall back to `99-default.link`, and the interfaces
will come up with kernel-default names (`enp1s0`, `enx*`, etc),
breaking the bond config and every service that references the
`ethernet0` / `ethernet1` names.

## Surviving cuems-common upgrades

These files are dpkg conffiles. On upgrade, if you've customized them
for your hardware, dpkg will prompt:

```
Configuration file '/etc/systemd/network/10-ethernet0.link'
 ==> Modified (by you or by a script) since installation.
 ==> Package distributor has shipped an updated version.
   What would you like to do about it ?
```

**Pick `N` (keep your version)** unless you know the new defaults
match your hardware. Picking `Y` (install package version) on a
host that doesn't match the N97 default WILL break interface naming
on the next reboot.

`dpkg -i --force-confnew` is the same outcome, applied silently.
Never use it on a non-N97 host without verifying.

## Recovering from a broken install

If a force-confnew install (or a wrong `Y` answer to the conffile
prompt) has corrupted your `.link` files:

```sh
# Inspect the actual PCI topology
for iface in ethernet0 ethernet1 wifi0; do
    [ -e /sys/class/net/$iface ] && \
        echo "$iface: $(readlink -f /sys/class/net/$iface/device | sed 's|.*/||')"
done
# (Or for whichever names the system assigned — check `ip -br link`.)

# Re-write the .link files with the correct paths
sudo $EDITOR /etc/systemd/network/10-ethernet0.link
# (set Match=Path=pci-<your-actual-path>)

# Don't reboot until verified — udevadm test-builtin shows what udev would do:
sudo udevadm test-builtin net_setup_link /sys/class/net/ethernet0 2>&1 | grep ID_NET_LINK_FILE
# Should print /etc/systemd/network/10-ethernet0.link, NOT 99-default.link
```

## Why this is a known sharp edge

`Match=Path=` ties the .link to a specific PCI BDF. There is no
host-portable matching directive that works across the fleet for
"the NIC the operator wants called ethernet0" — `MACAddress=` is
per-device, `Driver=` doesn't discriminate between two NICs of the
same chip, `OriginalName=` matches what the kernel called the device
before renaming (also topology-dependent).

The long-term fix is to auto-generate per-host .link files at
postinst time from `/sys/class/net/*/device` (so every host gets a
correct file regardless of what the package ships). Tracked
separately. Until then, this README is your safety net.
